{
  "id": "Compound::DB06203 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Alogliptin, a dipeptidyl peptidase 4 (DPP4) inhibitor, is linked to Cyp3a4 through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Alogliptin, as part of the DPP4 inhibitor class, binds to and influences the activity of Cyp3a4 via its inclusion in the broader class of DPP4 inhibitors, which includes sitagliptin and linagliptin. These inhibitors are known to interact with Cyp3a4, a key enzyme in drug metabolism. Additionally, Alogliptin's interaction with Flucloxacillin, which binds to Cyp3a4, provides an alternative route for the relationship between Alogliptin and Cyp3a4. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential mechanism by which Alogliptin may affect Cyp3a4, possibly through metabolic interactions or enzyme inhibition. The evidence supports a role for Alogliptin in modulating Cyp3a4 activity, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
